.SH KEY DESIGN DECISIONS
.SH Fork\-per\-session model
Each accepted client connection is handled in a forked child process.
This provides complete isolation between clients: a crash, resource
leak, or runaway command in one session cannot affect the server or
other sessions.
The trade\-off is that shell state (variables, functions, directory
changes) persists within a session but is isolated from other sessions.
.SH Protocol auto\-detection
The server detects the client\(cqs protocol version from the first byte
received on a new connection:
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII letter (A\-Z, a\-z) indicates v1 text protocol
.IP \(bu 2
Binary byte with value 0\-5 indicates v2 binary framing
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{\f[R] (0x7B) indicates v2 NDJSON wire format
.PP
This allows a single server to serve clients speaking any protocol
version without configuration.
.SH Channel multiplexing (v2)
The v2 protocol multiplexes six logical channels over a single
connection, identified by a single\-byte channel ID (0\-5):
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CHAN_CONTROL (0)\f[R]: Session lifecycle (auth, ping, configure,
disconnect)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CHAN_COMMAND (1)\f[R]: Command execution (eval, execute pre\-parsed
AST)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CHAN_STATE (2)\f[R]: Shell state operations (variables, functions,
aliases, traps)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CHAN_OBSERVE (3)\f[R]: Server\-push observability events
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CHAN_DEBUG (4)\f[R]: Interactive debugger (breakpoints, stepping,
AST inspection)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CHAN_PTY (5)\f[R]: Terminal emulation (PTY spawn, I/O relay,
resize, signals)
.PP
See \f[B]bash\-server\-channels\f[R](7) for details.
.SH Pre/post command hooks
The server registers hooks into Bash\(cqs command execution pipeline to
intercept commands before and after execution.
These hooks drive both the observability system (CHAN_OBSERVE) and the
debugger (CHAN_DEBUG).
.SH ANSI escape stripping
PTY mode includes a stateful finite automaton that strips ANSI escape
sequences from terminal output.
This allows clients to receive clean text without terminal formatting
codes, useful for programmatic consumption of interactive shell output.
